<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   What leaves this device in a Google backup or a device-to-device transfer, written out
   deliberately rather than left to the platform default.

   Both blocks name the same two domains, and that sameness is the decision: the shares this
   phone holds for other people ride along, so that switching phones does not silently destroy
   somebody else's redundancy. It is safe because a single share is information-theoretically
   independent of its secret, and because the private keys are not here at all — the Keystore
   will not export them.

   These are <include> rules rather than <exclude> rules on purpose. Anything stored outside
   filesDir and SharedPreferences stays behind until somebody adds it here, which is the point:
   a new store has to be classified rather than swept in.

   What a stolen backup would yield, and why it is accepted, is in deposplit.com/docs/security.md
   under "Data at rest, and what a backup carries".
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="file" path="." />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="file" path="." />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
